            <ArticleTitle>Central sensitization phenomena after third molar surgery: a quantitative sensory testing study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>116-27</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Surgical removal of third molars may carry a risk of developing persistent orofacial pain, and central sensitization appears to play an important role in the transition from acute to chronic pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this study was to investigate sensitization (primarily central sensitization) after orofacial trauma using quantitative sensory testing (QST).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 32 healthy men (16 patients and 16 age-matched control subjects) underwent a battery of quantitative tests adapted to the trigeminal area at baseline and 2, 7, and 30 days following surgical removal of a lower impacted third molar.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Central sensitization for at least one week was indicated by significantly increased pain intensity evoked by intraoral repetitive pinprick and electrical stimulation (p&lt;0.05) including facilitation of temporal summation mechanisms (p&lt;0.05), extraoral repetitive electrical stimulation (p&lt;0.001), significantly more frequent aftersensation in patients (p&lt;0.001), extraoral hyperalgesia due to single pinprick stimulation (p&lt;0.05) and larger pain areas due to intranasal stimulation (p&lt;0.001). Peripheral sensitization was indicated by intraoral hyperalgesia due to single pinprick (p&lt;0.05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We found clear signs of sensitization of the trigeminal nociceptive system for at least one week after the surgery. Our results indicate that even a minor orofacial surgical procedure may be sufficient to evoke signs of both central and peripheral sensitization, which may play a role in the transition from acute to chronic pain in susceptible individuals.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
